    "q-80123": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80123",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-4,-1,5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-4x-2y+2z=16.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-1$ and $y=2$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-2y+2z & =16 \\\\ -4(-1)-2(2)+2z & =16 \\\\ 2z & =16 \\\\ z & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-1,2,8)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,2,8⟩-⟨-4,-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-4,-2,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-12-6+6|}{\\sqrt{16+4+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,-12\\,|}{\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
