    "q-129119": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129119",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $Q(1,-3,7)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨6,3,-2⟩=-17$, find the shortest distance from $P(2,-6,2)$ to $Π.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-3,7⟩-⟨2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨6,3,-2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 6+3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6+9-10|}{\\sqrt{36+9+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-7|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
