    "q-129158": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129158",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1,3,-4)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $6x+2y+3z=9.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=0$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+2y+3z & =9 \\\\ 6(1)+2(0)+3z & =9 \\\\ 3z & =3 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,0,1)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,0,1⟩-⟨-1,3,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨6,2,3⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 6+(-3)\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12-6+15|}{\\sqrt{36+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|21|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
